<!--
  MergeTree part retention

  ClickHouse makes one PART per INSERT, and this node inserts one row at
  a time. Measured on the live node under load: about 1,400 parts a
  minute across four tables. The merges keep up; what does not is the
  DISK, because a part that has been merged away is kept for
  `old_parts_lifetime` before deletion - eight minutes by default.

  At steady state that retains roughly rate x lifetime parts, and their
  bytes are dominated by merge OUTPUT, which is rewritten as the tables
  grow. The result measured here: 6.8 MiB of live data sitting under 5.5
  GB of dead parts, growing 245 MB a minute. Eighty free gigabytes would
  have lasted about five hours.

  The retention window exists so a replica can still fetch a part that
  was merged away mid-transfer, and so a restart can roll back to a
  known part set. This deployment is a single node with no replicas, so
  thirty seconds is enough to cover an in-flight read while cutting the
  retained volume sixteenfold.

  This does not fix the cause, which is one insert per row. It removes
  the consequence, which was going to fill the disk before anyone could
  fix the cause.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <merge_tree>
        <old_parts_lifetime>30</old_parts_lifetime>
        <!-- Sweep more often than the default second-granularity walk,
             since there are thousands of candidates rather than a few. -->
        <merge_tree_clear_old_parts_interval_seconds>5</merge_tree_clear_old_parts_interval_seconds>
    </merge_tree>
</clickhouse>
